    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d246">

                <pb facs="0482" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Austria)/11–344:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gallman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-03T19:00:00+01:00">November 3, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 3—6:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9549. Department’s 9178, November 2, 7 p.m.<anchor n="96" xml:id="d246-f1"/>
                    Directive from CCS to SACMED enclosed with Department’s instruction 4535 has not
                    been presented to the EAC.</p>
                <p>On October 4 Strang wrote to Mr. Winant suggesting that they send Gousev
                    identical letters summarizing the pertinent parts of that directive. Strang
                    later withdrew this suggestion in favor of that outlined in Embassy’s 8840
                    (October 17, 6 p.m., Comea 113).<anchor n="97" xml:id="d246-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gallman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d246-f1" n="96" xml:id="d246-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it stated that the Department did not wish its instruction No. 4535
                    of September 14 (printed on page 460), presented to the European Advisory
                    Commission (740.00119 Control (Austria)/11–244).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d246-f2" n="97" xml:id="d246-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the British Foreign Office felt that it would be
                    desirable to approach the Russians with a proposal that as soon as any part of
                    Austria was occupied by any of the Allied armies, a tripartite control
                    commission should be set up (740.00119 Control (Austria)/10–1744).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
